    "available_at": "2026-10-24T20:23:03.681582+00:00",
    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nNew Hot Wheels Red Line Club Exclusive 240Z Looks Like Double the Trouble\n17 photos\nPhoto: Mattel\nOne of my wife’s coworkers invited us to lunch today, and I knew I couldn’t go there empty handed. I asked if their son liked cars, thinking I’d give him one of my premium, carded Hot Wheels collectibles. I don’t think he liked it, as he seemed more like a FIFA enthusiast. Oh well, I know what I can get him the next time.\nIt can feel a bit mind boggling to see that most people don’t really care about tiny cars, especially when I’m so caught up in the topic on a daily basis. I guess kids these days don’t even pay attention to real-world vehicles either, as they’re just a means of transportation to them.\nI’m not sure if I should blame the car companies, the ride-sharing companies or the Social Media platforms for the current situation. I assume identifying the “culprit” wouldn’t make a difference anyway. In a way, they’re probably better off this way, as this hobby can be quite expensive and time consuming.\nThat’s true if you do manage to pick your battles carefully. Take for instance what’s happening in the Red Line Club series. While Overdrive seemed like the perfect solution to a problem that has been a nuisance for years, it won’t guarantee you can get every single RLC on a yearly basis.\nThe introduction of the Chase cars has made the game even more complicated than before. We’ve seen three of them so far in 2026: the Porsche 911 Turbo 3.3 Targa, the 1948 Chevrolet Fleetline and the '21 Ford Bronco.\nBuying all three of them from eBay could cost as much as $2,500, which seems downright insane if you ask me. The Bronco is the cheapest one of the bunch, and I expect the new Datsun 240Z will be quite a bit more desirable by comparison.\nThis is the same casting that Phil Riehlman designed for the 2006 First Edition collection, where it debuted alongside other memorable castings such as the Ferrari 512 M and the Bone Shaker. Yes, that’s right. This is the kind of RLC model that exists across multiple series, which means it doesn’t feature any opening parts.\nThat’s a huge downside to me, but I guess other people don’t mind the situation. Why would it still be in production after two decades otherwise? The 240Z first big moment happened in 2011 when it joined the Super Treasure Hunt series, and it went one step further in 2013 with only 3,500 units showing up for the HWC.com Special Edition release.\nWe have seen four other similarly variations since, and you might remember it even showed up as a replica of Larry Chen’s Ole Orange Bang earlier this year for Boulevard . While Spectraflame Aqua looks like a good match for the regular version of the RLC release, I’m not a fan of the lettered tires (which isn’t something I’d normally say).\nMattel will only be making 5,000 units of the Polished Chrome Chase, and this is one battle you might want to stay away from if you care about your sanity and your wallet. But hey, who am I to stop you from trying? The action starts on October 27 , 2026 at 9 AM PT, and you can only order two items per customer, for $28 a pop. I imagine it will all be over in under 60 seconds, so good luck with it.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHot Wheels Red Line Club Datsun datsun 240z Hot Wheels Datsun Hot Wheels Chase\nAbout the author: Dragos Chitulescu\nThe things Dragos enjoys the most in life are, in no particular order: cars, motorcycles, diecast cars, and drifting. He's seen (and driven) many vehicles since he started his writing career back in 2009, but his garage currently houses a 1991 Mazda RX-7 FC3S Turbo II and a 1999 Suzuki SV650-S. Full profile\nUP NEXT USC Football Star Takes His Dodge Charger Hellcat to a Tuner, Drives Back in a Different Car",
    "body_sha256": "280c0df12cee2451339f6a458dda72ba60eedbe148301870150d19526c4bdbb7",
